---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ImagePlaceholder from '../../components/ImagePlaceholder.astro';
import { getProject } from '../../data/projects';

const project = getProject('multifamily-search-optimization');

const sections = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'challenge', label: 'Challenge' },
  { id: 'role', label: 'My role' },
  { id: 'approach', label: 'Approach' },
  { id: 'execution', label: 'Execution' },
  { id: 'qa', label: 'Quality assurance' },
  { id: 'screens', label: 'Process artifacts' },
  { id: 'outcome', label: 'Outcome' },
  { id: 'why-it-matters', label: 'Why it matters' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  project={project}
  sections={sections}
>
  <section class="cs-section" id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Most SEO case studies describe one website. This one describes a method applied across many at
      the same time. As a Marketing Specialist on the SEO and digital strategy team at REACH by
      RentCafé / Yardi, I lead digital strategy across a portfolio of 40+ client websites — and I'm
      the person on that team who handles the technical end of it: structured data, implementation
      and QA. At one point the portfolio was approximately 46 properties, with onboarding and
      optimization running at roughly 20 properties per month.
    </p>
    <p>
      At that volume the deliverable isn't a single optimization. It's a repeatable process that
      produces market-specific work without becoming either generic or unmanageable.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted" style="font-size:var(--step--1);">
      This case study describes method and scope only. It contains no client data, property-level
      performance figures or confidential information; examples are anonymized.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="challenge">
    <h2>Challenge</h2>
    <p>
      Multifamily search is deceptively hard. Each property is a separate local business competing in
      its own submarket, with its own amenity set, pricing structure, policies and renter questions —
      but the websites share platforms and templates, and the team supporting them is shared too.
    </p>
    <p>The real constraints:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Scale versus specificity.</strong> Templated content ranks poorly and reads badly; fully bespoke content for every property doesn't fit the time available.</li>
      <li><strong>Local competition.</strong> Every property competes against nearby communities, large listing aggregators, and the map pack.</li>
      <li><strong>High turnover of facts.</strong> Availability, pricing structures, specials, policies and management details change constantly, which creates drift between the website, listings and the Google Business Profile.</li>
      <li><strong>Onboarding volume.</strong> New properties arrive continuously and each needs a correct baseline before any optimization is meaningful.</li>
      <li><strong>Mixed stakeholder fluency.</strong> Recommendations go to property managers and regional teams who don't work in search and need a reason, not a checklist.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="role">
    <h2>My role</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Search strategy per property</strong> — intent research, priority pages, content direction for the specific submarket</li>
      <li><strong>On-page optimization</strong> — titles, meta descriptions, heading structure, body content, internal linking, image optimization, calls to action</li>
      <li><strong>Technical implementation</strong> — schema markup, metadata, crawl and indexation checks, page-level technical fixes</li>
      <li><strong>Local search</strong> — Google Business Profile optimization and entity/NAP consistency</li>
      <li><strong>GEO / AEO work</strong> — structuring property information and FAQs so AI search and answer systems can interpret and cite it correctly</li>
      <li><strong>Analytics</strong> — Google Search Console and GA4 analysis, performance reporting, prioritized recommendations</li>
      <li><strong>Property onboarding</strong> — establishing the search baseline for new properties entering the portfolio</li>
      <li><strong>QA</strong> — verifying implementation across desktop and mobile before anything was considered done</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="approach">
    <h2>Approach</h2>
    <p>
      The method below is the thing I actually built. It's sequenced so that each step's output is the
      next step's input, which is what makes it repeatable at volume.
    </p>

    <h3>1. Onboarding baseline</h3>
    <p>
      For each new property: confirm the entity facts (name, address, contact, management, policies,
      amenities), check indexation and crawlability, verify analytics and Search Console access, and
      establish the local footprint. No optimization starts before the baseline is correct, because
      optimizing on top of wrong facts just distributes the error.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Market and intent research</h3>
    <p>
      Identify how renters in this specific submarket search: neighborhood and landmark language,
      unit and bedroom phrasing, amenity priorities, and the question set that comes up for this
      property type and price point. This is where templated approaches fail — the vocabulary is
      genuinely local.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Page-level optimization plan</h3>
    <p>
      Map intent to pages so each priority page owns one job: the community overview, floor plans,
      amenities, neighborhood, and the contact or tour action. Then write the metadata and content
      direction for each.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Implementation</h3>
    <p>
      Titles and meta descriptions, heading hierarchy, body content, internal links between related
      pages, descriptive image alt text, schema markup, and calls to action placed where the decision
      actually gets made.
    </p>

    <h3>5. Local and entity work</h3>
    <p>
      Google Business Profile optimization, category and attribute accuracy, and reconciling the
      property's facts across the website and its public listings. This is also the foundation of the
      AEO work — answer systems lean heavily on consistent local entity data.
    </p>

    <h3>6. FAQ and answer coverage</h3>
    <p>
      Build FAQ content from the questions renters actually ask — pet policies, parking, application
      requirements, lease terms, utilities, neighborhood specifics — written as self-contained answers
      so they work for a reader scanning the page and for a system extracting a response.
    </p>

    <h3>7. Measure and iterate</h3>
    <p>
      Search Console for query and impression behavior, GA4 for what happens after arrival. The
      output is a short prioritized list per property, with the reasoning attached, rather than a
      dashboard nobody reads.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="execution">
    <h2>Execution</h2>
    <p>Work implemented repeatedly across the portfolio:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Unique, intent-matched title tags and meta descriptions at page level</li>
      <li>Semantic heading structure on priority templates</li>
      <li>Body content reflecting submarket vocabulary and amenity priorities</li>
      <li>Internal linking between community, floor plan, amenity and neighborhood pages</li>
      <li>Schema markup for property and local business information</li>
      <li>Descriptive, non-redundant image alt text</li>
      <li>FAQ sections structured for direct answer extraction</li>
      <li>Google Business Profile optimization and entity consistency fixes</li>
      <li>Calls to action aligned to where renters are in their decision</li>
      <li>Search Console and GA4 analysis with prioritized next steps</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="qa">
    <h2>Quality assurance</h2>
    <p>
      QA is the step that makes volume possible without quality collapsing. For each property I
      verified:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Metadata rendered as intended, with no truncation or duplication across pages</li>
      <li>Headings form a correct hierarchy and the page's subject is unambiguous</li>
      <li>Structured data validates and matches the visible page content</li>
      <li>Internal links resolve and point at the intended page</li>
      <li>Images carry useful alt text and are sized appropriately</li>
      <li>Layout, navigation and CTAs behave correctly at mobile and desktop widths</li>
      <li>Entity facts match between the website, the Google Business Profile and listings</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="screens">
    <h2>Process artifacts</h2>
    <p class="text-muted" style="font-size:var(--step--1);">
      Anonymized examples to be added. Replace with screenshots that contain no client-identifying
      or confidential information.
    </p>

    <ImagePlaceholder
      label="Onboarding checklist"
      description="The property onboarding checklist, anonymized — the baseline sequence applied to every new property."
      path="/images/projects/multifamily-seo/onboarding-checklist.png"
      caption="Onboarding baseline: the same sequence every time, which is what makes volume survivable."
    />

    <ImagePlaceholder
      label="Page optimization map"
      description="Intent-to-page mapping for a representative property, anonymized."
      path="/images/projects/multifamily-seo/page-optimization-map.png"
      caption="Each priority page owns one job."
    />

    <ImagePlaceholder
      label="Search Console analysis"
      description="Query and impression analysis view with property-identifying details removed."
      path="/images/projects/multifamily-seo/search-console-analysis.png"
      caption="Search Console analysis — anonymized before publishing."
    />

    <ImagePlaceholder
      label="FAQ structure"
      description="Example FAQ block structured for direct answer extraction, anonymized."
      path="/images/projects/multifamily-seo/faq-structure.png"
      caption="Self-contained answers: readable on the page, extractable by an answer engine."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="outcome">
    <h2>Outcome</h2>
    <p>Confirmed scope and capabilities:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Managed a portfolio of approximately 46 properties at one point, each with its own market and competitive set</li>
      <li>Worked onboarding and optimization at a cadence of roughly 20 properties per month</li>
      <li>Built a documented, repeatable onboarding and optimization method rather than case-by-case work</li>
      <li>Implemented the technical layer directly — schema, metadata, internal linking, image optimization — and QA'd it</li>
      <li>Extended the standard SEO method to cover GEO/AEO requirements: entity consistency and answer-ready content structure</li>
      <li>Translated Search Console and GA4 data into prioritized recommendations for non-specialist stakeholders</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Portfolio-level performance results: <span class="todo">[ADD VERIFIED RESULT]</span> — organic
      visibility, impression, click or lead metrics to be added only where they can be verified and
      shared without disclosing client information.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="why-it-matters">
    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <p>
      Working at this volume changed how I approach search work generally. When a decision has to be
      correct forty-six times, you stop reasoning case by case and start designing the process: what
      gets checked, in what order, with what evidence, and how a second person could verify it.
    </p>
    <p>
      It's also where the GEO work became concrete rather than theoretical. Entity consistency,
      canonical facts and self-contained answers aren't abstractions in multifamily — they're the
      difference between an answer engine confidently describing your property and quietly naming a
      competitor instead.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
